The problem asks us to find the number of distinct pairs of mochi $(A, B)$ such that $A$ can be placed on top of $B$.
We are given $N$ mochi with sizes $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_N$.
The mochi are sorted in ascending order, i.e., $A_1 \le A_2 \le \dots \le A_N$.
We can place mochi $A$ on top of mochi $B$ if and only if $A \le \frac{B}{2}$.
We need to count the number of pairs of indices $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ is placed on top of $A_j$ (meaning $A_i$ is the top one and $A_j$ is the bottom one) satisfying the condition.
